.Dd August 19, 2026
.Dt CROSSOVER-WHEEL 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm crossover-wheel
.Nd force feedback for the Thrustmaster T150 under CrossOver
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
gives a Thrustmaster T150 working force feedback in games running under
CrossOver on macOS, without installing anything system wide: no kext, no
DriverKit system extension, no SIP change and no AMFI change.
.Pp
macOS enumerates the wheel as an ordinary joystick once it is in firmware
mode, and CrossOver carries it into the bottle, with one caveat this
project had to diagnose: the SDL library CrossOver 26 bundles drops
Thrustmaster wheels, and the bottle needs
.Ev SDL_JOYSTICK_HIDAPI Ns =0
in its environment before the wheel appears at all. With it set, games see
the wheel again. RESEARCH.md A35, B10 and B11.
.Pp
The install also turns hidraw off in that bottle, a
.Li DisableHidraw
value under
.Li HKLM\eSystem\eCurrentControlSet\eServices\eWineBus ,
because with hidraw on the wheel has been measured missing from the bottle
entirely. winebus reads it when the bottle boots, so it takes effect at the
bottle's next start, and it is the bottle rather than the wheel: every device
whose only copy came from the IOHID bus loses it, a wheelbase with its own
force feedback included. The installer's checkbox, and
.Fl -keep-hidraw ,
leave the setting alone for a bottle that needs it. RESEARCH.md A25 and B12.
.Pp
Force feedback itself is missing for a structural reason: the wheel's
descriptor contains no Physical Interface Device collection, and Wine
performs no force feedback translation of its own on macOS. A wheelbase
that brings its own PID collection needs no translation and already works
through CrossOver's hidraw path; the T150 brings none, which is the gap
this project fills. RESEARCH.md B12.
.Pp
Rather than trying to present a force feedback device to macOS, which is
blocked by restricted entitlements no free path can obtain,
.Nm
presents it inside the bottle. A proxy
.Pa dinput8.dll
wraps DirectInput's force feedback surface and forwards effects to an
unprivileged macOS daemon that owns the wheel through
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport .
.Sh COMPONENTS
.Bl -tag -width "t150-dinput8.dll"
.It Xr t150d 8
The macOS daemon. Owns the wheel, encodes effects, writes output reports.
The protocol, the slot table, the downgrades and the watchdog work, and the
HID backend has opened a real wheel unprivileged; its shutdown packets
visibly stopped a runaway effect, so its writes reach the firmware. It has
rendered effects a client asked for, a constant force and a damper, and
both were felt on the wheel.
.Fl n
swaps the backend for a log, which is how the rest of the stack is exercised
without hardware.
.It Nm t150-dinput8.dll
The in-bottle proxy. It loads in a real bottle, chain-loads CrossOver's
builtin, and carries a game's force feedback: Assetto Corsa creates its
effects through it and the wheel renders them. It is installed
as
.Pa dinput8.dll
in the bottle's
.Pa system32 ,
with a copy of CrossOver's builtin from
.Pa lib/wine/x86_64-windows
beside it as
.Pa dinput8_orig.dll
to chain-load, and a
.Li native,builtin
override for
.Li dinput8
in the bottle's registry. The copy has to be that one: the
.Pa dinput8.dll
already in
.Pa system32
is a placeholder that Wine will not load as an implementation. It must go in
.Pa system32
rather than beside a game, because SDL reaches DirectInput through
.Fn CoCreateInstance ,
which resolves to an absolute
.Pa system32
path. It is an x86_64 PE and serves 64-bit games only, since a 32-bit process
is redirected to
.Pa syswow64 .
The README has the whole procedure.
.Pp
It also gives games one instance GUID for the wheel and translates it back on
every call that takes one. DirectInput derives that GUID from the order the
device arrived in the bottle, and this wheel arrives again on every replug,
every wake and its own switch out of boot mode, so a game's saved button
mapping did not survive any of them: the wheel came back under its own name
as a device the game had never seen. RESEARCH.md B14.
.It Xr t150boot 8
One-shot boot to firmware mode switch, unprivileged. Has switched a real
wheel and confirmed it back at its firmware id.
.It Xr t150ctl 1
Rotation range, gain and autocenter from the command line, unprivileged. Has
identified a real wheel and sent it settings without error; whether the
range visibly changed went unrecorded.
.It Xr t150-probe 1
The four measurement tools. They are what proved the wheel obeys.
.El
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
The T150 renders constant force, square, triangle, sine, both sawtooths,
spring and damper in hardware, all eight named by Thrustmaster's own driver.
Friction, inertia and ramp are not in its protocol. Refusing those outright
risks a game disabling force feedback altogether, so the daemon downgrades
them instead: friction and inertia to damper, and ramp to a time-sliced
constant. Square and triangle were downgraded to sine until the vendor's
driver gave their type codes.
.Pp
The daemon does not write to the wheel from the frame that arrives. A frame
records what a slot should hold and a pass, at most one every 4 milliseconds,
sends whatever differs from the bytes that slot last put on the wire. Only
effect parameters are treated that way: starts, stops, settings and every
path that makes the wheel safe go out at once. See
.Xr t150d 8 .
.Pp
Nothing in this stack learns that a game has exited: Wine's
.Pa hidclass.sys
consumes the close at the physical device object, and DirectInput only sends
a reset on a graceful unacquire. A crashed game would therefore leave the
last commanded force latched on the wheel. The daemon treats silence as a
fault and stops every effect after half a second without traffic.
.Sh STATUS
Everything is written and everything has now run somewhere real:
.Nm t150boot
switched a wheel,
.Nm t150ctl
talked to one, the daemon opened one unprivileged and stopped a runaway
effect on its way out, and the proxy loaded in a CrossOver bottle and
chain-loaded the builtin behind it. Effects have since crossed the loopback
and been felt on the wheel, first from the in-bottle probe with no game
involved and then from a game: Assetto Corsa creates its effects through the
proxy and drives a T150 through the whole stack, which is what the project was
built to do.
.Pp
The measurement the project was gated on has been made and it succeeded: an
unprivileged
.Fn IOHIDDeviceSetReport
sets the wheel's autocenter to full and it becomes hard to turn, then
releases it and it turns freely, with CrossOver free to keep reading the
wheel throughout. Force feedback followed once the wheel's input was opened
with
.Li 42 04 ,
which nothing on macOS sends and which the daemon now sends itself. A
constant force and a periodic both move the wheel, through the HID pipe and
through interrupt OUT. See
.Pa docs/PROBES.md
and RESEARCH.md A19, A28 and A29.
.Pp
Everything written so far is checked by
.Cm make test
on any machine: golden vectors for the encoders, round trips for the
protocol, and the daemon driven through simulated time and over a real
socket. That proves the bytes are the ones
.Pa docs/PROTOCOL.md
describes, and nothing at all about whether the wheel honours them.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr t150ctl 1 ,
.Xr t150-probe 1 ,
.Xr t150boot 8 ,
.Xr t150d 8
.Pp
.Pa docs/HANDOFF.md ,
.Pa docs/RESEARCH.md ,
